For no-builtin CASE=0 GCC warns about the int-to-pointer conversion then fails
to link alloca; Clang rejects the conversion. CASE=1 has undefined alloca in
both toolchains. Builtin CASE=2 and CASE=4 are binding conflicts, not agreed
values. A focused fix must not claim that the two oracles agree on these.

Additional command: COMPILER -std=gnu11 -O0 -DCASE=CASE
                    dynamic-stack-names.c -o /tmp/names; /tmp/names
Both compilers reject CASE=0 (implicit declaration); both compile and return 0
for CASE=1 (extern prototype). GNU89's implicit builtin recognition must not
silently admit an undeclared ordinary call in the GNU11 profile.
